# @boundary rejects too few arguments to 'add': expected 2, found 1
#
# BUG-232 REGRESSION GUARD. Authored 2026-07-30 as a `@boundary-pending` probe
# recording SILENTLY WRONG OUTPUT: `print("${add(1)}")` emitted no diagnostic at all,
# while the identical call as a statement warned.
#
# It has since changed severity TWICE in one day, and caught both moves itself:
#   silent  -> warns    when BUG-232 was fixed (the walker reached interpolation)
#   warns   -> rejects  when BUG-142 promoted arity to a hard error
# The second was not anticipated when the first was written. A probe that pins a
# diagnostic's TEXT also pins its SEVERITY, which is worth knowing before you write one.
#
# Third pending tripwire to fire on a real fix, after BUG-234 and BUG-236.
#
# THE BUG WAS MUCH LARGER THAN ITS TITLE. `checkCallsInExpr` branches over Expr and
# handled 8 of 34 variants; interpolation was the visible symptom of a walker missing
# nearly every container (list/set/array/tuple literals, dicts, ternaries, orelse,
# catch, slices, optional chains, chained comparisons, `except`, `is`, `old`). Fifteen
# arms were added, then a sixteenth once BUG-237 explained why interpolation resisted:
# `StringPart` was not in TypeChecker's `use Ast exposing ...` list, so codegen never
# registered its boxed variants and the `^Expr` payload emitted as a raw pointer.
#
# The file name is now a small lie -- it is no longer "unchecked". Kept anyway: the
# bug-fixture gate and BUGS.md both reference it, and a rename buys nothing a comment
# does not.
def add(a: int, b: int): int
    return a + b

def one(a: int): int
    return a

def main()
    # Both are now hard errors. The `rejects` kind asserts by substring, so the header
    # pins too-few; too-many is pinned by the sibling bv_arity_too_many probe.
    print("tooFew=${add(1)}")
    print("tooMany=${one(1, 2)}")
